<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Myriokephalon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1176">1176</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Myriocephalum · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-seljuk-wars">Byzantine–Seljuk wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Myriokephalon was fought on 17 September 1176 in the mountains of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, near Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egirdir">Eğirdir</a>. The Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan II</a> ambushed the army of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a> in a narrow pass as it marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>. Manuel escaped and agreed a truce, but the defeat ended Byzantine hopes of recovering central Anatolia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-myriokephalon.jpg" alt="Battle of Myriokephalon" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crusades_surprised_by_turks.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
